WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:04.540
Horror fans rejoiced when The Nun was announced
in June 2016.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.920
Billed as the "darkest chapter" in the Conjuring
universe, the film is the fifth installment

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.080
in the fast-growing horror franchise... and
it centers around a demonic character featured

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.139
briefly in The Conjuring 2 and Annabelle:
Creation.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.980
"I have survived many terrifying encounters,
but nothing could have prepared me for this."

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.400
From the character's origins in real-life
demon lore to spooky behind-the-scenes stories

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.760
from the cast and crew, this is the untold
truth of The Nun.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.600
From music to mayhem

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.730
British director Corin Hardy didn't have tons
of experience when director and producer James

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.900
Wan asked him to join the Conjuring Universe
team.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.000
But Hardy's 2015 feature film debut The Hallow
proved he was capable of great things.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.260
The consensus on Rotten Tomatoes is that The
Hallow promised a "wonderfully horrifying

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.120
future for director Corin Hardy."

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.880
Prior to The Hallow, Hardy had been plying
his trade in music videos.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.980
Between 2007 and 2011, the director created
hallucinatory clips for bands like The Prodigy.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.900
His very first music video even hinted at
an interest in the macabre.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.500
The video for "She Is the New Thing" by the
Horrors is a refreshingly creepy clip… but

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.400
bigger, creepier things were on the way...

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:22.900
Strange bedfellows

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:30.060
"... therefore, I seal that which was not
to be said."

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:35.390
Based on Umberto Eco's bestselling novel,
the 1986 melodrama The Name of the Rose stars

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.340
Sean Connery as William of Baskerville.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.460
The fictional friar is often thought to be
a mash-up of Sherlock Holmes and Middle Ages

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.100
philosopher William of Ockham.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.820
Christian Slater plays Baskerville's apprentice,
and both men are investigating the unexplained

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.000
death of a famous monk.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.800
The plot thickens when the last person who
spoke to the monk is also found dead in a

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.520
vat of pig blood, and the residents of the
abbey fear the devil is at work.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.600
James Wan told The Hollywood Reporter that
The Nun is like The Name of the Rose crossed

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.520
with The Conjuring.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.880
The similarities between the stories are clear.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.580
Both films have a religious historical setting,
and both involve a member of the church arriving

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.020
at a strange place to solve a mysterious murder.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.920
One major difference: In The Nun, a supernatural
force is actually behind the mayhem.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.420
Get to know Valak

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.520
The Nun is a product of James Wan's imagination,
but the demonic being is rooted in real-life

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:23.020
lore.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.620
The demon's name gives us power over it."

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.260
In the classic occult text The Lesser Key
of Solomon, Valak is given the title Grand

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:34.500
President of Hell and assumes the form of
a winged child riding a two-headed dragon.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.660
That sounds epic, but maybe not for a haunted-house
movie.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.640
So far, Valak has taken multiple forms in
The Conjuring universe.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.360
In fact, the demon briefly becomes the Crooked
Man in The Conjuring 2:

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.611
Does this mean Ed and Lorraine Warren will
eventually have a final showdown with a creepy

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.780
kid on a dragon?

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.640
Uh, let's hope not.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.500
"Banned" equals buzz

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.860
In the early days of YouTube, jumpscare videos
were all over the place.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.140
Like a throwback to those simpler times, a
controversial seven-second ad appeared on

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.580
YouTube a few weeks before The Nun's release
date.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.060
First, a volume icon appeared in the middle
of the user's screen.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.600
Viewers were only given a few seconds before
a shrieking nun popped up to scare the bejeezus

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:30.180
out of them.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.860
The ad didn't sit well with everyone.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.060
One Twitter user even suggested it could be
dangerous, writing:

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.600
"If you see an ad on YouTube with the volume
sign being turned down and nothing else, it's

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.840
a jump-scare for the new Nun movie coming
out….

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.340
Please retweet to save a life."

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:48.400
The warning was retweeted over 100,000 times,
forcing YouTube to respond.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:53.220
The company removed the ad for violating its
"shocking content" policy... which was ultimately

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.160
fantastic publicity for the film.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:56.400
Thanks, jumpscare.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:58.160
Truth or scare?

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.730
During her Comic-Con interview with Entertainment
Tonight, Taissa Farmiga explained why she's

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.820
frightened by the occult:

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.739
"I don't know what I believe about supernatural
and ghosts, but I'm the kind of person who's

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:10.919
like, 'Why open the doors, why let it in,
because then I'll get an answer, and I don't

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.169
want a definite answer."

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.000
But one person who really seems to believe
in ghosts is Corin Hardy.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.260
In fact, he told CinemaBlend he saw two real
ghosts while filming a scene in a Romanian

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:21.760
fortress.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:27.240
"So I go into the room As I went into the room, I see these two
guys from the crew, you know, from the sound

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.920
department or something, you know, just sitting
a little further back in the dark."

00:04:30.129 --> 00:04:34.240
Hardy says he greeted the men, and then sat
with his back to them while getting his shot…

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.080
but the real terror was yet to come.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.060
Half an hour later, he turned around:

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.400
"And there was just no one in the room.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.340
And there hadn't been anyone in there at all,
you know?

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.680
There was no way they could have come out..."

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.820
We can only guess what happened next.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:49.840
"Tell me who you are?"

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.940
"Mama, mama?"

